from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "3. The Twelve"),
("p", "Claim: the Twelve were chosen by the Lord in his earthly ministry, named apostles, sent to the house of Israel and promised twelve thrones in the kingdom. Their office belongs to Israel’s Prophecy Program. The volume reads the passages that choose them, the qualification Peter states when a place falls vacant, and the promise of the thrones, and it keeps the wall of the city in Revelation 21 apart from the argument."),
("sub", "3.1 The choosing"),
("p", "Luke records that the Lord, when it was day, “called unto him his disciples: and of them he chose twelve, whom also he named apostles” (Luke 6:13). Mark records the purpose in two clauses: “that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,” and “to have power to heal sicknesses, and to cast out devils” (Mark 3:14, 15). The choosing is the Lord’s. The men did not offer themselves, and the Twelve were chosen from a larger company of disciples."),
("tbl", "The choosing of the Twelve.", [
    ("Luke 6:13", T("Luke 6:13"), "The Lord chooses twelve of his disciples and names them apostles."),
    ("Mark 3:14, 15", T("Mark 3:14-15"), "Two purposes: to be with him, and to be sent to preach with power over sickness and devils."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.2 The sending"),
("p", "The sending is stated with its limits. “These twelve Jesus sent forth, and commanded them, saying, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not” (Matthew 10:5). The next verse gives the field: “But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.” The Twelve are sent to Israel, in the land, in the days of the Lord’s ministry. The first thing the passages say of the office is its audience."),
("tbl", "The sending of the Twelve.", [
    ("Matthew 10:2", T("Matt 10:2"), "The twelve are named apostles, and the first is Simon, who is called Peter."),
    ("Matthew 10:5, 6", T("Matt 10:5-6"), "They are sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, and not to the Gentiles or the Samaritans."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.3 The qualification"),
("p", "When Judas had fallen, Peter stood among the disciples and said that the place must be filled from among the men “which have companied with us all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, beginning from the baptism of John, unto that same day that he was taken up from us” (Acts 1:21, 22). The purpose he names is a witness: “must one be ordained to be a witness with us of his resurrection.” The qualification is to have been with the Lord from John’s baptism to the ascension, and the office is a witness to the resurrection."),
("p", "The lot fell on Matthias, “and he was numbered with the eleven apostles” (Acts 1:26). The volume cites the choosing of Matthias and leaves its argument to Acts Commentary Volume 1. What matters here is the statement of the qualification. It shuts out anyone who had not been with the Lord through his ministry, and it ties the office to the Lord’s earthly ministry and to Israel."),
("tbl", "The qualification and the choosing.", [
    ("Acts 1:21, 22", T("Acts 1:21-22"), "The witness must have been with the Lord from John’s baptism to the ascension."),
    ("Acts 1:25, 26", T("Acts 1:25-26"), "Matthias takes the place from which Judas fell and is numbered with the eleven apostles."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.4 The promise of the thrones"),
("p", "The Lord says to the Twelve, “Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of man shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel” (Matthew 19:28). In the upper room he repeats the promise: “And I appoint unto you a kingdom, as my Father hath appointed unto me; that ye may eat and drink at my table in my kingdom, and sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel” (Luke 22:29, 30)."),
("p", "Two things stand in the promise. The thrones are in the kingdom, when the Son of man sits in the throne of his glory, and they are over the twelve tribes of Israel. The promise belongs to the Prophecy Program, and it is made to the men who followed the Lord. The volume draws from it one conclusion: the office of the Twelve looks forward to a day in the kingdom, and it is the kingdom that fills it."),
("tbl", "The promise of the thrones.", [
    ("Matthew 19:28", T("Matt 19:28"), "Twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel, in the regeneration."),
    ("Luke 22:29, 30", T("Luke 22:29-30"), "A kingdom appointed to them, and thrones judging the tribes."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.5 The wall of the city"),
("p", "The Revelation shows “the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb” (Revelation 21:14). The verse names the twelve apostles of the Lamb on the foundations of the city. The volume reports it as written. It does not say that the foundation of Ephesians 2:20 and the foundations of the city are one thing, and it does not use the one to interpret the other. Ephesians speaks of the household of God, and Revelation of a city whose gates bear the names of the tribes of the children of Israel."),
("p", "This is as far as the volume goes with the verse. The Revelation is addressed to the servants of Jesus Christ in the setting its first verses name, and the volume assigns the book to Israel’s line. Whether the twelve foundations and the twelve thrones are the same honour is a question the passages do not settle."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The Twelve were chosen by the Lord and named apostles, and they were sent to the house of Israel.",
    "The qualification stated in Acts 1:21, 22 is to have been with the Lord from John’s baptism to the ascension, and the office is a witness of the resurrection.",
    "The Lord promised the Twelve twelve thrones over the twelve tribes in the kingdom.",
    "The office is Israel’s and looks to the kingdom. Revelation 21:14 is reported and not made to interpret Ephesians 2:20.",
  ]),
]
